#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205074} :activity/id #uuid "dec8292b-04ba-57b4-8720-afabf5599b8a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050839 :topic/id #uuid "6abf9723-c4e0-5904-b4e9-49c86041a14f" :topic/math-academy-id 5289} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205206 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we learned that the principal components of a standardized data matrix $X$ are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix, and that these eigenvectors appear as the columns of $V$ in the singular value decomposition $X = U\\Sigma V^T.$ We also learned that the variance along each principal component is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to use principal components to perform **feature extraction** — the process of projecting high-dimensional data onto a lower-dimensional subspace while preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nTo illustrate the idea, suppose a biologist has recorded the resting heart rate and breathing rate for four mammals of different sizes. Both measurements have been standardized, giving the $4 \\times 2$ data matrix\n\n![](images/c6/c6952e27be521fed69a3e9ff76520b01873fdc3e3e28f0015550cbdc8cda3110.svg)\n\nThe biologist knows that body size strongly influences both heart rate and breathing rate — smaller animals tend to have faster rates for both. They want to extract a single \"metabolic index\" that captures this underlying factor. How should they proceed?\n\nFrom the SVD $X = U\\Sigma V^T,$ the first principal component is\n\n![](images/82/82a177ec2b7448d62d171a249e464d5da15308e41a6140b156d2d50d2c251f00.svg)\n\nwhich points in the direction of maximum variance. To obtain a one-dimensional representation of the data, we project each row of $X$ onto this direction by computing\n\n![](images/39/39fa24e5c2dbec4ceb7a56c0ee17f9c4996d08a2d9e1bc91d0849d7e24abe5c1.svg)\n\nThe result is a $4 \\times 1$ vector — one number per animal — that captures the variation in body size. The second animal, with the most negative standardized values (indicating a larger, slower-metabolizing animal), has the largest projected value." :tutorial/id #uuid "894cce51-8da3-55a0-8188-37b91ca5c47e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13253 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "95bf8e11-91d4-5faa-b4b9-f34fccb4a50e" :step/math-academy-id 629550 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205075}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205075 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205213 :tutorial/content "More generally, suppose we have a standardized $n \\times p$ data matrix $X,$ where each row is a data point and each column is a feature. To reduce the data from $p$ dimensions to $k$ dimensions, we project the data onto the first $k$ principal components. If $V_k$ is the $p \\times k$ matrix whose columns are the first $k$ principal components, the reduced data matrix is\n\n$$\nZ = X V_k.\n$$\n\nEach row of $Z$ is a $k$-dimensional representation of the corresponding data point, expressed in terms of the principal component directions.\n\nThere is an equivalent way to compute $Z$ using the SVD. Starting from $X = U\\Sigma V^T,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =X{V}_{k} \\\\  & =UΣ{V}^{T}{V}_{k} \\\\  & =UΣ[\\begin{aligned}{I}_{k} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U[\\begin{aligned}{Σ}_{k} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={U}_{k}{Σ}_{k},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $U_k$ is the $n \\times k$ matrix of the first $k$ columns of $U,$ and $\\Sigma_k$ is the $k \\times k$ diagonal matrix of the first $k$ singular values. \n\nSo, we can compute the reduced data either as $XV_k$ or as $U_k\\Sigma_k.$\n\n**Watch out!** The vector $v_1$ (the first column of $V$) is a *direction* — a unit vector in feature space pointing along maximum variance. By contrast, the product $Xv_1$ (equivalently, $u_1\\sigma_1$) is the *projected data* — an $n \\times 1$ vector giving each data point's coordinate along that direction. Some texts refer to this projected data as \"the first principal component of the data\" or as \"scores,\" so the term \"principal component\" can mean either the direction or the projected values, depending on context." :tutorial/id #uuid "41d5d02d-cb8d-54bf-9edc-b6d69f78b42e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14748 :tutorial/title "The Projection Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7cac9d17-5846-58de-95e6-7e0f8e56e0ac" :step/math-academy-id 629551 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204105 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203875 :question/id #uuid "d6f2c4b7-eb2d-5d32-bcd8-8ec9443c14da" :question/math-academy-id "e-18786"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de17e8f9-e3dc-5ba0-b6c9-327aa8cc10e0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050539}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529136}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Best One Dimensional Reduction Given SVD of Data"} :step/id #uuid "74cf39f4-5e68-568e-bccb-a900bb795de7" :step/math-academy-id 629552 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205214 :tutorial/content "In the previous section, we saw how to project data onto the first $k$ principal components. But how do we choose $k?$ Keeping more components preserves more information, while keeping fewer gives a more compact representation at the cost of discarding some variation.\n\nRecall that the variance along the $i$th principal component is proportional to $\\sigma_i^2,$ where $\\sigma_i$ is the $i$th singular value. Therefore, the fraction of total variance preserved by keeping the first $k$ components is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2 + \\sigma_2^2 + \\cdots + \\sigma_k^2}{\\sigma_1^2 + \\sigma_2^2 + \\cdots + \\sigma_p^2} = \\dfrac{\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sigma_i^2}{\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{p} \\sigma_i^2}.\n$$\n\nWhile the amount of variance we need to preserve depends on the application, as a rule of thumb, we will choose the smallest $k$ that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance. That is, we select $k$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sigma_i^2}{\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{p} \\sigma_i^2} \\geq 0.90.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "97dbcb11-96a6-5a5f-aafc-7d212236c53c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14749 :tutorial/title "Choosing the Number of Components"} :step/id #uuid "0f3228b2-720c-5166-8dbe-667a26a03e21" :step/math-academy-id 629555 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204106 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203876 :question/id #uuid "a17de4fa-f7c3-5038-b9fa-03780393b360" :question/math-academy-id "e-18840"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bbf7902-3a15-5dd0-b2c8-e5b7fd541345" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543515}] :knowledge-point/title "Choosing the Amount of Dimensionality Reduction for a Given Dataset"} :step/id #uuid "e5823a48-e0f6-565c-9756-9e43c2763e07" :step/math-academy-id 629556 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205215 :tutorial/content "We can now summarize the complete pipeline for using PCA to extract features from a dataset.\n\n1\\. **Standardize the data.** Subtract the mean of each feature and divide by its standard deviation. This ensures that all features contribute equally to the analysis, regardless of their original units or scales.\n\n2\\. **Compute the SVD.** Find the singular value decomposition $X_{\\text{std}} = U\\Sigma V^T.$ The columns of $V$ are the principal components, and the diagonal entries of $\\Sigma$ are the singular values.\n\n3\\. **Choose the number of components.** Using the singular values, compute the cumulative variance for each possible choice of $k$ and select the smallest $k$ that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance.\n\n4\\. **Project the data.** Compute the reduced data matrix $Z = X_{\\text{std}} V_k,$ where $V_k$ consists of the first $k$ columns of $V.$\n\nTo interpret results in terms of the original features or to apply the same transformation to new data, we must keep track of the means and standard deviations used in Step 1." :tutorial/id #uuid "3cf2cdec-09e3-5344-84d2-4617e6830871" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14750 :tutorial/title "The Feature Extraction Pipeline"} :step/id #uuid "cc9242f2-052f-51db-9396-bda401bd5870" :step/math-academy-id 629559 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204107 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203877 :question/id #uuid "dcbf83b0-b552-52d6-97d3-9bc3ac6b0823" :question/math-academy-id "e-18844"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45f11c96-89e5-5662-81b6-06cff80a4cf2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543563}] :knowledge-point/title "Using PCA to Dimensionally Reduce an Uncentered Dataset"} :step/id #uuid "f79c44a0-cf86-5bde-a18b-20df7fdcba79" :step/math-academy-id 629560}] :activity/title "Feature Extraction Using PCA" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204154}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543632} :activity/id #